-- title:
--   Frobenius exponent of a G-point is invariant under base change
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring with a distinguished element $\pi$, let $r$ be a prime, and let $Onr$ be a nontrivial commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$. Assume $Onr$ satisfies `hOnr_closed`: every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a value $\mathrm{eval}\,x\,p$ lying in the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, for some $x \in Onr$; and assume `hFr`: $\mathrm{Fr}(x) - x^{r}$ lies in that same ideal for every $x \in Onr$. Let $M$ be a moduli package for $r$ over $Onr$, i.e. an assignment, to each commutative ring $B$ together with a ring homomorphism $Onr \to B$ and a witness that $r$ is nilpotent in $B$, of a type, functorial in $B$ along ring homomorphisms compatible with the structure maps. Let $B$ and $L$ be commutative $\mathcal O$-algebras with $L$ nontrivial, let $\psi : Onr \to B$ and $\varphi : B \to L$ be $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphisms, and suppose the image of $\pi$ in $L$ is nilpotent. Let $x_B$ be a $G$-point of $M$ over $B$ and $x_L$ one over $L$ — each consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map out of $Onr$ (its leg), a witness that $r$ is nilpotent in the base, and a point of $M$ over that data — and suppose $x_L$ is the pushforward of $x_B$ along $\varphi$, whose leg is $\varphi$ composed after the leg of $x_B$. If the leg of $x_B$ equals $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{k_0}$ and the leg of $x_L$ equals $(\varphi \circ \psi) \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{j}$ for integers $k_0, j$, then $j = k_0$.
--
--   This is the rigidity of the Frobenius exponent (the "parity") attached to a point of the Drinfeld-type moduli functor: the twisting exponent recorded on a leg cannot change when the point is specialised along an $\mathcal O$-algebra map to a nonzero base in which $\pi$ is nilpotent. It is used in the construction and comparison of rigidified fake elliptic curves, where a globally chosen exponent over a base must be matched with the exponents read off on individual pieces.
